rAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2120c37f4da2","stem":"5 Acids, Bases and Buffers 80","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A buffer is any acid and base which together control the concentration of pH in the blood."},{"label":"B","text":"A buffer is the solution which allows CO2 to be lost from the lungs in order to control the pH of the lungs."},{"label":"C","text":"A buffer is a weak base and its acid salt or a weak acid and its basic salt. The ratio of the two components helps maintain blood pH levels."},{"label":"D","text":"A buffer is a mixture of two acids which together help to maintain blood pH"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-9cb4ff8d4eff","stem":"Neutralization of an acid by a base can be represented by the following equation: H O OH H O 3 2 2 + − + → This means that the pH of the solution after the neutralization is approximately","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"6"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"9 Answer is B: The solution resulting from a neutralisation reactions should have pH = 7 (should be neutral) – provided that it is properly titrated."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags"