d_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-38ecf041a347","stem":"Which of the following amino acids play an important role in the interaction of histone proteins with the DNA?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-36d9e1c993aa","stem":"Which of the following areas of protein structure involves the interactions between separate protein chains?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e4329c71e77f","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a component of amino acids that contributes to protein structure?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d9f26a8f1498","stem":"Which of the forces below that stabilize the tertiary structure of proteins is the only one that is a covalent bond?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-40412f1732f6","stem":"What is the actual stage in which Vitamin C and molecular oxygen are needed during collagen maturation?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Nutrition and Dietetics","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Nutrition and Dietetics","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-95757bec5a32","stem":"INFLAMMATORY RESPONSE The inflammatory response is observed when a pathogen is able to break anatomical barriers and enter our body. Inflammation is recognized as redness, swelling, pain and loss of function in the infected area. It is triggered by chemicals such as histamine and cytokines that are secreted by injured cells or immune system cells such as macrophages. These chemicals recruit tissue-repairing proteins and pathogen-destroying leukocytes to the site of injury for pathogen killing and tissue repair. Some cytokines are anti-viral in nature such that they block the protein synthesis in host cells which is a prerequisite for the virus to survive and divide. REFERENCES: 1. https://www.google.com/search?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3eef8e54aae3","stem":"A 40 year acute myeloid leukemia patient with hyperleukocytosis and poor karyotypic features failed to go to remission after two courses of induction. He partially responded to anti CD33 Mylotarg “Ozagamicin”. He has a monozygotic twin brother, two fully matched siblings and many family members who are one antigen mismatch. Patient was advised for peripheral stem cell transplantation. To give the best chance for cure in this patient, what type of stem cell transplantation you will chose?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematological Malignancies","topicOriginal":"Hematological Malignancies","subtopic":"Leukemia and Lymphoma","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematological Malignancies","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3d8dfd78d440abc669e079bdb46faef204c2ff7d"},{"id":"q-f61fd0e380cf","stem":"Which one is not true for chronic lymphocytic leukemia treatment: A. Rai stage IV patients benefit from steroid before proper chemotherapy B All Rai stage I & II patients should be immediately treated C. Fludarabine, Chlorambucil, Vincristine & Prednisolone are effective D. Coomb’s positive hemolytic anemia patients better be given steroid first E. Acyclovir should be used liberally for zoster and herpes simplex","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematological Malignancies","topicOriginal":"Hematological Malignancies","subtopic":"Leukemia and Lymphoma","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematological Malignancies","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e8cd77af0b2d","stem":"Which one of the following is regarded as \"HIGH GRADE\" lymphoma: A. Small lymphocytic Lymphoma B. Lymphocyte Rich Hodgkin Lymphoma C. Mantle cell Lymphoma D Lymphoblastic Lymphoma E. Lympho-plasmacytic Lymphoma","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematological Malignancies","topicOriginal":"Hematological Malignancies","subtopic":"Leukemia and Lymphoma","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematological Malignancies","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cf5b861c064f","stem":"Which of the following is correct : A The average life span of the normal red cell is 60 days B The hemoglobin molecule contains two globin chains C In normal individuals most red cells are destroyed in the bone marrow D. Red cells principally metabolize glucose E. The average half-life for decay of radioactive chromium labeled normal red cells is around 30 days","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Transfusion Medicine","topicOriginal":"Transfusion Medicine","subtopic":"Blood Grouping and Compatibility","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Transfusion Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-34e05a0fe426","stem":"Which of the following statements are correct : A 15% of the male population are Rhesus positive B Rhesus positive males are always homozygous C Naturally occurring Anti-A agglutinin causes marked hemolysis D. The indirect Coomb's test is of value in cross-matching of blood E Transfusion of Rhesus incompatible blood is only safe in males Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ 40","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Transfusion Medicine","topicOriginal":"Transfusion Medicine","subtopic":"Blood Grouping and Compatibility","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Transfusion Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1517d560086a","stem":"Which of the following diseases may be associated with purpura?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1e5dd1694081","stem":"Which one of the following statements relate to Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia: A. Results from proliferation of Myeloblasts B. Mature and maturing white blood cells are increased C. It is the commonest cancer in children. D. Blasts are positive for Sudan Black E. Never cured by chemotherapy alone","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematological Malignancies","topicOriginal":"Hematological Malignancies","subtopic":"Leukemia and Lymphoma","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematological Malignancies","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7ffff1eee294","stem":"You are provided with a table to comment on the hemostatic test results for some common bleeding disorders. Write “P” if you think the test is prolonged and “N” for a normal test result. To help you, many of the answers are already correctly labeled. The tests are: P.T. meaning Prothrombin Time","type":"True / False","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"P.T.T. meaning Activated Partial Thromboplastin Time"},{"label":"B","text":"T. meaning Bleeding Time T.T. meaning Thrombin Time The bleeding diseases are: vWD meaning von Willebrand’s Disease DIC meaning Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation ITP meaning Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura Hemophilia which includes both Hemophilia A & B DISEASE OR FACTOR DEFICIENCY PT APTT TT"},{"label":"B","text":"T. HEMOPHILIA N vWD N N or P N XII & XI N N N VII N X, V & II N FIBRINOGEN P P N LIVER FAILURE P P N DIC ITP Select True (T) and False (F) answers."}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"de08dc4592af7f86a6820ec35cba20209f94e45a"},{"id":"q-594fa3bf94d2","stem":"Which of the following are often found in the blood after splenectomy?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7bc148f8527bc42c775bb55603345f6ec3bbcc3e"},{"id":"q-92a0001a8311","stem":"All, EXCEPT one, of the following relate to laboratory features of hemolysis","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Transfusion Medicine","topicOriginal":"Transfusion Medicine","subtopic":"Blood Grouping and Compatibility","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticulocytosis is a usual finding"},{"label":"B","text":"Polychromasia usually detected"},{"label":"C","text":"Coomb’s test is positive in all patients."},{"label":"D","text":"Cross-matching might be difficult if autoantibodies are present"},{"label":"E","text":"Mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia is usual LEUKEMIA IMMUNOPHENOTYPING EXMINATION: There are five main immunological types of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemias: Common ALL; T-ALL; Pro-B ALL; Pre-B ALL and B-ALL. Each of these types of ALL is characteristically positive for certain markers. The main immunological markers used are TdT; HLA-DR; CD10; Cyt.; SmIg; CD 19 to 22, which are Pan B cell markers and CD 2 to 8 markers, which are T cell markers. In the following table, you are requested to fill in the positive tests with the plus (+) sign and the negative tests with the minus (-) sign. For your convenience, I have already marked few of the tests. ALL TdT CD10 Cyt.  SmIg CD19-22 CD 2-8 (B- Lineage) (T-Lineage) Q371 Pro-B Q372 cALL - Q373 Pre-B Q374 B-ALL - Q375 T-ALL Professor Anwar Sheikha, MD, FRCP, FRCPath., FACP One Thousand Hematology MCQ 50 Select the True (T) & False (F) answers:"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Transfusion Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"7bf6e32b4e2f9cbc5fc683aeaa915aefbb508ebe"},{"id":"q-f56dcba17433","stem":"Which of the following are consistent with diagnosis of chronic iron deficiency?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"c37873d7e58d677c6622599f12665c78e1f9570e"},{"id":"q-83858b168ec3","stem":"When treating patients with iron deficiency anemias: A. Iron should be given as a second-line measure because blood transfusion is more effective and a quicker mode of therapy B. Iron should be given until the hemoglobin is corrected C. Iron should not be given beyond the correction of the anemia to avoid iron overload D Iron should be continued beyond anemia correction to replenish the stores E. Parenteral iron is a quicker, better and safer mode of therapy","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Transfusion Medicine","topicOriginal":"Transfusion Medicine","subtopic":"Blood Grouping and Compatibility","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Transfusion Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cc5c9cd68f00","stem":"When two persons with β- thalassemia minor get married, the chance of having children with β- thalassemia major is: A. 1 in 2 B 1 in 4 C. 1 in 8 D. All the children E. None of the children","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4e13d0575c26","stem":"When a normal person gets married to a patient with β- thalassemia minor, the chance of having children with β- thalassemia major is: A. 1 in 2 B. 1 in 4 C. 1 in 8 D. All the children E None of the children","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-152d1b49a9a6","stem":"When a normal person gets married to a patient with β- thalassemia minor, the chance of having children with β- thalassemia minor is: A 1 in 2 B. 1 in 4 C. 1 in 8 D. All the children E. None of the children.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-46d5df0b239c","stem":"When collecting and processing blood samples from patients, the technologist must follow all these rules, EXCEPT: A. Always recap the syringe before its disposal B. Wear disposable gloves C. Never re-use the same syringe for another patient D. Seek immediate attention if injured or contaminated by blood E Do not dispose sharp objects like needles, syringes and lancets with other things","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"