S. Endemic T. Epidemic U. Exoskeleton V. Facultative Parasite W Host X. Hypostome Y. Hysterosoma Z. Idiosoma AA. Immunity BB. Incidence CC. Intensity DD. Intermediate host EE. Larvae FF. Lice comb GG. Lyme's disease HH. Macrophage II. Maggot JJ. Malathion KK. Mange LL. Masking MM. Mutalism NN. Myiasis OO. Nit PP. Nymph QQ. Opisthosoma RR. Parasitism SS. Parentenic Host TT. Pedipalps UU. Phoresis VV. Plague WW. Plasma cell XX. Predation YY. Prevelence ZZ. Proboscis AB. Prosoma AC. Proterosoma AD. Prothorax AE. Pupae AF. Pygidium AG. Pyrethrin AH. Reservoir Host AI. Resilin AJ. Scabies AK. Scutum AL. Siphon.... AM. Spiracle AN. Stylets AO. Symbiosis AP. Temporary host AQ. T-cell AR. Thorax AS. Uniramous AT. Vector AU. Warbles AV. Western encephalitis AW. West Nile Virus AW. Zoonosis For questions 37-42, match the word with its best definition. (2 points each)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","too_many_options"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a6cdd657c502","stem":"A friend of yours stops by for a visit and tells you that he found a tick embedded in his thigh. He explains that while pulling it out, the “head” broke off and remains in his skin. He was warned by his mom that he should not “leave the head” when removing a tick, and he is concerned about the outcome of this mistake. Please explain to your friend the following (20 total points)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Why is the term “head” not correct in this context? (3 points)"},{"label":"B","text":"On the back of this sheet, include a ventral perspective drawing of a female Ixodes tick, and label the following structures: hypostome, anus, spiracular plate, pedipalps, carapace (scutum), capitulum and walking legs. (10 points)"},{"label":"C","text":"Explain to your friend how to determine if this is a “Lyme Disease tick”. Include the appropriate structure(s) on your drawing. (3 points)"},{"label":"D","text":"Explain how your friend should have removed the tick. (4 points) NAME ________________________________________ SDSU GENERAL PARASITOLGY: Zool 467 LECTURE EXAM #1: SEPTEMBER 16, 2005 MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS Mark the most appropriate answer for each question listed below on the appropriate answer sheet. (2 points/question)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"99b4e090866f48c7e08b8a3b0a308973173c8a55"},{"id":"q-3de172956ec5","stem":"West Nile Virus is primarily transmitted by ......","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simulium"},{"label":"B","text":"Culex"},{"label":"C","text":"Glossina"},{"label":"D","text":"Anolpheles"},{"label":"E","text":"Aedes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-df79e800e195","stem":"Black flies or buffalo gnats .........","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"are of medical and/or economic importance because their larvae cause myiasis in humans and domestic animals"},{"label":"B","text":"are in the family Simuliidae"},{"label":"C","text":"are in the family Muscidae"},{"label":"D","text":"breed in temporary ponds"},{"label":"E","text":"are restricted to tropical areas of the world NAME ________________________________________ SDSU GENERAL PARASITOLGY: 467 LECTURE EXAM #1: SEPTEMBER 16, 2005 MATCHING WORD BANK FOR MATCHING"},{"label":"A","text":"Adult stage"},{"label":"B","text":"African Sleeping Sickness"},{"label":"C","text":"Anal groove"},{"label":"D","text":"Anthelminthic"},{"label":"E","text":"Antibody"},{"label":"F","text":"Antigen"},{"label":"G","text":"Antigenic Variation"},{"label":"H","text":"B-cell I. Bots J. Capitulum (gnathosoma) K. Chagas Disease L. Chelicerae M. Chiggers N. Commensalism O. Complement P. Ctenidium Q Definitive R. Density S. Ectoparasite T. Endoparasite U. Endemic V. Epidemic W. Exoskeleton X. Facultative Parasite Y Host Z. Hypostome AA. Hysterosoma BB. Idiosoma CC. Immunity DD. Incidence EE. Intensity FF. Intermediate GG. Larvae HH. Lice comb II. Lyme's Disease JJ. Macrophage KK. Maggot LL. Malaria MM. Malathion NN. Mange OO. Masking PP. Mutalism QQ. Myiasis RR. Nit SS. Nymph TT. Opisthosoma UU. Parasitism VV. Parentenic WW. Pedipalps XX. Phoresis YY. Plague ZZ. Plasma cell AB. Predation AC. Prevelence AD. Proboscis AE. Prosoma AF. Proterosoma AG. Prothorax AH. Pupae AI. Pygidium AJ. Pyrethrin AK. Reservoir AL. Resilin AM. Rocky Mountain Spotted Fever AN. Scabies AO. Scutum AP. Siphon.... AQ. Spiracle AR. Stylets AS. Symbiosis AT. Temporary AU. T-cell AV. Thorax AW. Tularemia AX. Typhus AY. Uniramous AZ. Vector BC. Warbles BD. Western Encephalitis BE. West Nile Virus BF. Yellow Fever BG. Zoonosis NAME ________________________________________ SDSU GENERAL PARASITOLGY: 467 LECTURE EXAM #1: SEPTEMBER 16, 2005 MATCHING For questions 21-31, match the word with its best definition. (2 points each)"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d144fb0cd51db9169fa51685d113537208c7fead"},{"id":"q-09bfcef991bb","stem":"Diagram the basic immune response to an infectious agent (30 points). The diagram must represent or describe the roles of the following immune components","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antibodies"},{"label":"B","text":"B-cells"},{"label":"C","text":"helper T-cells"},{"label":"D","text":"macrophages"},{"label":"E","text":"plasma cells"},{"label":"F","text":"compliment"},{"label":"G","text":"cytotoxic T-cells"},{"label":"H","text":"interleukins i. Class II major histocompatibility complex coded protein j. memory cells NAME ________________________________________ SDSU GENERAL PARASITOLGY: 467 LECTURE EXAM #1: SEPTEMBER 16, 2005 ESSAY SDSU GENERAL PARASITOLGY: 467 LECTURE EXAM #1: SEPTEMBER 16, 2005 ESSAY"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","too_many_options"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d4fd7009d9f00e722f5036ec88514a5e400531cb"},{"id":"q-22cc597e64d6","stem":"Identify the distinguishing characteristic for each of the following suborders, and provide an example of a member from each suborder (Yes, you may use common names for the examples) [12 points]","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Astigmata"},{"label":"B","text":"Mesostigmata"},{"label":"C","text":"Prostigmata"},{"label":"D","text":"Metastigmata"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8d1faf287019","stem":"While visiting your aunt’s house for Thanksgiving, you notice a small insect crawling on your nephew’s head. Last Christmas, your nephew received a small stereomicroscope and supplies (e.g. slides, coverslips, forceps, etc.) from Santa. [20 points]","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Describe what you would look for to determine if this was a head louse (5 points)"},{"label":"B","text":"Explain to your aunt how Dave acquired the infestation and what that says about Dave’s level of hygiene (5 points)"},{"label":"C","text":"Explain what your aunt should do to get rid of these little pests on Dave and in her house. (5 points)"},{"label":"D","text":"Explain what Dave might do to minimize the chances of becoming infected again. (5 points) Parasitology Final Exam Nematodes as well as Comprehensive I. Identify the nematode. (20 points)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-490821785a7f","stem":"Strongyloides stercoralis VI. List the top ten parasites we have covered this semester and put them in rank order (#1 most important, etc.). Make up you list, taking into consideration, degree of pathology, mortality, incidence of infection worldwide, and general misery the organism causes. Do not use any genus more than once. (10 points) VII. A dichotomous key is a tool used to identify an unknown organism. The key proceeds from general to specific characteristics. It begins by providing alternative pathways using (usually) morphological characteristics. As an example, suppose you were studying an unknown microscopic protozoan (that actually was Paramecium) and you questioned its identify. You could identify it, using a key much as....... 1.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"cilia as locomotor organelles go to page 2"},{"label":"B","text":"flagella as locomotor organelles go to page 3"},{"label":"C","text":"pseudopodia as locomotor organelles go to page 4 2."},{"label":"A","text":"ciliature uniform go to page 5"},{"label":"B","text":"ciliature non-uniform go to page 6 3."},{"label":"A","text":"uniflagellate go to page 7"},{"label":"B","text":"multiflagellate go to page 8"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3cf4598a7f04","stem":"For the species listed below, be familar with each life-cycle and general regional distribution; be able to give a few examples of risk groups, be able to recognize the degree of pathology, examples of symptoms, and how you would diagnosis and infection with each.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trypanosoma brucei gambiense and Trypanosoma brucei rhodesience"},{"label":"B","text":"Trypanosoma cruzi"},{"label":"C","text":"Leishmania donavani"},{"label":"D","text":"cutaneous leishmaniasis"},{"label":"E","text":"Giardia intestinalis"},{"label":"F","text":"Trichomonas vaginalis"},{"label":"G","text":"Entamoeba histolytica"},{"label":"H","text":"Naegleria gruberi i. Sarcocystis j. Toxoplasma gondii k. Cryptosporidium parvum l. Plasmodium falciparum m. Plasmodium vivax n. Pneumocystis o. Isospora belli p. Acanthamoeba sp."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["too_many_options"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b5a57c93d0e6","stem":"Describe the method of transmission for each of the following in question #2.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditSta